AB  - PM2.5, as the main component of haze, is a serious hazard to human health. Accurate prediction of PM2.5 concentration can help the government to effectively reduce the damage caused by haze events. The prediction of PM2.5 concentration using deep learning models has gradually become a research hotspot. For predicting PM2.5 concentration considering the non-Euclidean distribution characteristics of air quality monitoring stations and the time-series characteristics of pollutant concentration data, a hybrid model combining multi-graph convolution and Long Short-Term Memory network (MGC-LSTM) was proposed in this study, based on the six pollutant concentration data of CO, NO2, O-3, PM10, PM2.5, and SO2. Two different methods are used in the study to construct adjacency matrices to build multiple graphs composed of stations, and then, the two matrices were fused into a new graph. We use the adjacency matrix of the new graph and Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) to extract spatial features of the input data, and then transfer the results of GCN into Long Short-Term Memory network (LSTM) to obtain the predicted values of PM2.5 concentration. This empirical study shows that the newly constructed model makes full use of spatiotemporal information and has higher prediction accuracy compared with that of the comparison model.
