AB  - Burning sacrificial offerings, a Chinese custom, can cause severe air pollution at some festivals, especially in urban areas. For example, during the Hanyi Festival (Festival) in 2020, the PM2.5 concentrations in Lanzhou, China, peaked at 20:00 (mean: 161 mu g/m(3); max: 353 mu g/m(3)) and remained high until 22:00. To eliminate the impact of meteorological variation, estimate the change in PM2.5 emission due to the incineration of sacrificial offerings (ISO), and assess the effectiveness of relevant controls, a random forest (RF) approach was utilized to quantify the meteorologically normalized contribution of ISO emissions (CISO MN) to the PM2.5 concentration during the Festival from 2015 to 2020 in Lanzhou. The maximum hourly CISO MN (C-ISO MNmax) during the Festival in 2017 at national sites in urban areas (mean: 11 mu g/m(3)) was lower than that in other years (mean: 12-66 mu g/ m(3)) due to strict controls at the end of the year as the Air Pollution Prevention and Control Action Plan was implemented. Nevertheless, the C-ISO MNmax (mean: 74 mu g/m(3); max: 339 mu g/m(3)) during the Festival in 2019 exhibited the highest contribution rate (mean: 60%; max: 87%) to PM2.5 at microsites in urban areas in the six years. The C-ISO MNmax at six national sites from 2015 to 2020 showed increasing trends, as was also observed at the microsites from 2017 to 2020, whether the sites were in urban or rural areas, indicating that the controls did not have persistent effects. Health effects attributed to ISO-related PM2.5 pollution in Lanzhou Valley were found to have increased mainly during the 2019 and 2020 Festivals compared with those in 2017 and 2018. Although ISO impacts increased as a whole, some areas suffering severe effects from ISO emissions exhibited considerably decreased rates of C-ISO MNmax, indicating the effectiveness of control measures in these areas. Therefore, good management, persistent controls, and modification of related habits are recommended to alleviate ISO pollution.
