AB  - A near-past, trend-attribute extraction technique is proposed for short-term hourly particulate matter (PM2.5) forecasting. Multiple attributes are extracted from the univariate PM2.5 time-series data for the prior 12 h to forecast PM2.5 for the next 12 h with the aid of machine/deep learning (ML/DL) algorithms. The method avoids the uncertainties of using a selection of exogeneous variables to forecast PM2.5 trends that tend to be highly volatile with erratically spaced peaks. Fifteen trend-decomposed attributes are extracted for each hourly data record of a 2016 to 2020 dataset for Nottingham Centre (a city in the United Kingdom). The 41979 data records are evaluated by sixteen ML/DL forecasting models. Each model is trained and validated with two datasets (2016-2018 and 2016-2019) that are then used to forecast 5 h-ahead periods (t0, t+1, t+3, t+6 and t+12) for 2019 and 2020, respectively. ML models outperform DL models in forecasting PM2.5 for 2019 and 2020, because the DL models are adversely impacted by suboptimal convergence and/or over-fitting. The best performing models are multiple linear regression (MLR) and support vector regression (SVR) that outperform traditional timeseries autoregressive models which estimate a parametric autocorrelation parameter, substantially so for time periods beyond t+1. The transparent MLR model reveals that all the trend-attribute variables considered influence the forecasts of the trained models, but their relative contribution varies according to the time period being forecast. Enabling the models to allocate different weights to each variable for different time period forecasts adds a useful dimension of flexibility to this method compared to autoregressive models.
